# ECMWF Vault API

`https://secrets.ecmwf.int` exposes a Vault-compatible HTTP API under `/v1/`.
Authenticate with a Vault token in the `X-Vault-Token` header. Access depends
on the token's policies and the path.

## Verify a token

The following Bash commands prompt for a token without putting it in shell
history or a command-line argument. They show selected token metadata only;
the complete lookup response can contain the token itself. Requires `curl`
and `jq`.

```bash
read -rs -p 'Vault token: ' VAULT_TOKEN; echo
printf 'header = "X-Vault-Token: %s"\n' "$VAULT_TOKEN" |
  curl -sS --fail-with-body --config - \
    https://secrets.ecmwf.int/v1/auth/token/lookup-self |
  jq '.data | {policies, ttl, renewable}'
```

Keep the token in the current shell only while needed; finish with
`unset VAULT_TOKEN`. Never commit tokens, include them in logs, or share them
in chat. Revoke a token if it has been exposed.

## Read a KV v2 secret

The KV v2 read endpoint is `/v1/<mount>/data/<secret-path>`; the secret fields
are in `data.data` in the JSON response. For example, after prompting for
`VAULT_TOKEN` as above, this checks the Bluster secret and displays only its
field names:

```bash
printf 'header = "X-Vault-Token: %s"\n' "$VAULT_TOKEN" |
  curl -sS --fail-with-body --config - \
    https://secrets.ecmwf.int/v1/groups/data/user_applications_and_services_team/bluster_service |
  jq '.data.data | keys'
```

On 2026-11-11, a test token successfully read this path under the `groups`
KV v2 mount (HTTP 200). The token also had write and delete capabilities on
the path; other tokens can have different permissions. Do not log or publish
the response if it contains secret values.

## Connectivity and authentication notes

- `GET /v1/sys/health` is an unauthenticated connectivity check. A healthy
  standby can return HTTP 429; this does not necessarily mean rate limiting.
- A 403 can indicate an invalid or expired token, or insufficient policy
  permissions for the requested path.
- ECMWF also documents interactive OIDC/Keycloak login with the Vault CLI.
  See [ECMWF Vault use cases](https://confluence.ecmwf.int/pages/viewpage.action?pageId=315557281)
  and the [Vault HTTP API reference](https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/api-docs).
